if ukrainians were hopeful russia might  soften its demands a 90-minute summit  made it clear that won't happen  we cannot stop the war if the country  which committed the aggression the  invasion does not have a desire to do so  said ukraine's foreign minister after  the meeting  two weeks in there's no movement toward  a cease-fire  and no progress on establishing  humanitarian corridors to help the  beleaguered citizens of ukraine escape  negotiation may be impossible because  russia's claims are so detached from  reality  we didn't attack ukraine proclaimed  russian foreign minister sergey lavrov a  situation threatening directly the  russian federation security was there he  said  lavrov dismissed concerns about civilian  casualties as pathetic outcries  he even suggested the russian bombing of  a maternity hospital was justified  claiming the building was occupied by  ukrainian radicals not women or doctors  on social media russian diplomats  falsely claimed these pregnant women  seen in the rubble were actually actors  russian forces are at the very least  operating with reckless disregard for  the safety of civilians as russian units  launch artillery and airstrikes into  urban areas  the defiance stretches all the way to  the kremlin where vladimir putin told  his cabinet a newly isolated russia  would emerge stronger than ever  ukraine's fierce resistance may still  upend those plans  the door remains open to a diplomatic  end to the war but ukraine is clear  surrender will never be an option  jackson prosco global news washington
 

